DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       nnaammeedd--cchheecckkzzoonnee checks the syntax and integrity of a zone file. It per‐
       forms the same checks as _n_a_m_e_d does when loading  a  zone.  This  makes
       nnaammeedd--cchheecckkzzoonnee  useful for checking zone files before configuring them
       into a name server.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       --dd     This option enables debugging.

       --hh     This option prints the usage summary and exits.

       --qq     This option sets quiet mode, which only sets an exit code to in‐
              dicate successful or failed completion.

       --vv     This option prints the version of  the  nnaammeedd--cchheecckkzzoonnee  program
              and exits.

       --jj     When  loading  a  zone file, this option tells _n_a_m_e_d to read the
              journal if it exists. The journal file name is assumed to be the
              zone file name with the string ..jjnnll appended.

       --JJ ffiilleennaammee
              When loading the zone file, this option tells _n_a_m_e_d to read  the
              journal from the given file, if it exists. This implies _-_j.

       --cc ccllaassss
              This  option  specifies the class of the zone. If not specified,
              IINN is assumed.

       --ii mmooddee
              This option performs post-load zone integrity  checks.  Possible
              modes  are  ffuullll  (the default), ffuullll--ssiibblliinngg, llooccaall, llooccaall--ssiibb‐‐
              lliinngg, and nnoonnee.

              Mode ffuullll checks that MX records refer  to  A  or  AAAA  records
              (both in-zone and out-of-zone hostnames). Mode llooccaall only checks
              MX records which refer to in-zone hostnames.

              Mode  ffuullll  checks  that  SRV records refer to A or AAAA records
              (both in-zone and out-of-zone hostnames). Mode llooccaall only checks
              SRV records which refer to in-zone hostnames.

              Mode ffuullll checks that delegation NS records refer to A  or  AAAA
              records (both in-zone and out-of-zone hostnames). It also checks
              that  glue address records in the zone match those advertised by
              the child.  Mode llooccaall only checks NS  records  which  refer  to
              in-zone  hostnames  or  verifies that some required glue exists,
              i.e., when the name server is in a child zone.

              Modes  ffuullll--ssiibblliinngg  and  llooccaall--ssiibblliinngg  disable  sibling   glue
              checks,  but  are  otherwise the same as ffuullll and llooccaall, respec‐
              tively.

              Mode nnoonnee disables the checks.

       --ff ffoorrmmaatt
              This option specifies the format of the zone file. Possible for‐
              mats are tteexxtt (the default), and rraaww.

       --FF ffoorrmmaatt
              This option specifies the format of the output  file  specified.
              For  nnaammeedd--cchheecckkzzoonnee,  this  does  not have any effect unless it
              dumps the zone contents.

              Possible formats are tteexxtt (the default), which is  the  standard
              textual  representation  of  the  zone, and rraaww and rraaww==NN, which
              store the zone in a binary format for rapid  loading  by  _n_a_m_e_d.
              rraaww==NN specifies the format version of the raw zone file: if NN is
              0,  the raw file can be read by any version of _n_a_m_e_d; if N is 1,
              the file can only be read by release 9.9.0 or  higher.  The  de‐
              fault is 1.

       --kk mmooddee
              This option performs cchheecckk--nnaammeess checks with the specified fail‐
              ure  mode.  Possible modes are ffaaiill, wwaarrnn (the default), and iigg‐‐
              nnoorree.

       --ll ttttll This option sets a maximum permissible TTL for the  input  file.
              Any  record with a TTL higher than this value causes the zone to
              be rejected. This is similar to using the mmaaxx--zzoonnee--ttttll option in
              _n_a_m_e_d_._c_o_n_f.

       --LL sseerriiaall
              When compiling a zone  to  rraaww  format,  this  option  sets  the
              "source serial" value in the header to the specified serial num‐
              ber. This is expected to be used primarily for testing purposes.

       --mm mmooddee
              This  option  specifies  whether MX records should be checked to
              see if they are addresses. Possible modes are  ffaaiill,  wwaarrnn  (the
              default), and iiggnnoorree.

       --MM mmooddee
              This option checks whether a MX record refers to a CNAME. Possi‐
              ble modes are ffaaiill, wwaarrnn (the default), and iiggnnoorree.

       --nn mmooddee
              This  option  specifies  whether NS records should be checked to
              see if they are addresses. Possible modes are  ffaaiill,  wwaarrnn  (the
              default), and iiggnnoorree.

       --oo ffiilleennaammee
              This  option  writes the zone output to ffiilleennaammee. If ffiilleennaammee is
              --, then the zone output is written to standard output.

       --rr mmooddee
              This option checks for records that are treated as different  by
              DNSSEC  but  are semantically equal in plain DNS. Possible modes
              are ffaaiill, wwaarrnn (the default), and iiggnnoorree.

       --ss ssttyyllee
              This option specifies the style of the dumped zone file.  Possi‐
              ble  styles are ffuullll (the default) and rreellaattiivvee. The ffuullll format
              is most suitable for  processing  automatically  by  a  separate
              script.   The relative format is more human-readable and is thus
              suitable for editing by hand. This does not have any effect  un‐
              less it dumps the zone contents. It also does not have any mean‐
              ing if the output format is not text.

       --SS mmooddee
              This option checks whether an SRV record refers to a CNAME. Pos‐
              sible modes are ffaaiill, wwaarrnn (the default), and iiggnnoorree.

       --tt ddiirreeccttoorryy
              This  option tells _n_a_m_e_d to chroot to ddiirreeccttoorryy, so that iinncclluuddee
              directives in the configuration file are processed as if run  by
              a similarly chrooted _n_a_m_e_d.

       --TT mmooddee
              This option checks whether Sender Policy Framework (SPF) records
              exist and issues a warning if an SPF-formatted TXT record is not
              also present. Possible modes are wwaarrnn (the default) and iiggnnoorree.

       --ww ddiirreeccttoorryy
              This option instructs _n_a_m_e_d to chdir to ddiirreeccttoorryy, so that rela‐
              tive  filenames in master file $$IINNCCLLUUDDEE directives work. This is
              similar to the directory clause in _n_a_m_e_d_._c_o_n_f.

       --DD     This option dumps the zone file in canonical format.

       --WW mmooddee
              This option specifies whether to check  for  non-terminal  wild‐
              cards.  Non-terminal wildcards are almost always the result of a
              failure to  understand  the  wildcard  matching  algorithm  (_R_F_C
              _4_5_9_2). Possible modes are wwaarrnn (the default) and iiggnnoorree.

       zzoonneennaammee
              This indicates the domain name of the zone being checked.

       ffiilleennaammee
              This is the name of the zone file.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEESS
       nnaammeedd--cchheecckkzzoonnee returns an exit status of 1 if errors were detected and
       0 otherwise.

